smoke-tutor: Because both routes match, but the router uses the most specific match [module]. A packet to `10.0.2.9` matches `0.0.0.0/0` (which matches everything), but it also matches `10.0.2.0/24`, which fixes the first 24 bits. Since `/24` is more specific than `/0`, router A sends it to the next hop `10.0.9.2` rather than the default route’s next hop `10.0.9.1` [module].
smoke-tutor: Think of `0.0.0.0/0` as a catch-all rule: “any address at all.” The route `10.0.2.0/24` is much more specific: it only means “addresses that start with `10.0.2`.” Since `10.0.2.9` starts with `10.0.2`, it fits that narrower rule, so the router chooses it and ignores the catch-all default route [module]. The router always prefers the most specific matching prefix [module].
